From c14537ce1ae3e4c95dca3e59a90754a14a6cae56 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: cruz <cruz@tbu.net>
Date: Mon, 24 Aug 2026 19:37:52 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 065/206] feat(shorebird): install the DD table at runtime --
 and run it

Closes the loop from the precompiler's Array of Code to the entry points
IndirectStaticCallInstr branches through. Their engine's dSYM carries the
whole contract, so almost none of this is inferred:

  Thread::indirect_static_call_table_      const uword*        thread.h:1685
  IsolateGroup::indirect_static_call_table_
                                  unique_ptr<unsigned long[]>  isolate.h:987
  IsolateGroup::indirect_static_call_table_size_   intptr_t    isolate.h:988
  Deserializer::ReadIndirectStaticCallTable()  app_snapshot.cc:1031, called
                                               from :7717

The field sits between the dispatch table's fields and saved_unlinked_calls_,
which is where ours now sits too. Thread's is a pointer, not the uword ours
was -- the emitted code cannot tell, but the ownership reads wrong otherwise.

ReadIndirectStaticCallTable is transcribed from their 0x741f1c..0x74204c: a
length, then one reference per slot, each resolved through refs and reduced
to entry_point_. A zero reference is fatal there rather than a stand-in for
"absent", which is why the precompiler now fills unclaimed slots with a
sentinel before serialization.

Their ObjectStore field name turned out to be recoverable after all, and the
comment claiming otherwise is gone: WriteRoots emits the field list as a
strtab, so their gen_snapshot spells out indirect_static_call_table_code_-
entries_ in list order, right where we had put ours. The same strtab shows a
selector_row_entries_ we do not have, noted where it matters.

Three bugs, all found by loading a snapshot rather than by reading one:

  * Slots could hold a Code that DiscardCodeObjects or Dedup had superseded.
    Pushing one as a root makes two Code objects share Instructions and trips
    CodeSerializationCluster::Insert. RefreshIndirectStaticCallTable re-points
    each slot after dedup. Theirs shows no such step; ours does not load
    without it.
  * FlowGraph::InsertMoveArguments picks the target by instruction type, and
    did not know this one, so target stayed null and every argument went on
    the stack while the callee expected registers. RangeError.checkValidRange
    read an address where a length belonged. InsertRecordBoxing had the same
    gap, behind an UNREACHABLE.
  * linker.cc uses Class::allocation_stub(), which the AOT runtime does not
    have, so dartaotruntime had never linked. Guarded; BuildKeyMap is only
    reachable from the precompiler.

End to end, with our own gen_snapshot and dartaotruntime on their fixture:

  no mapping                                   0 sites   correct output
  _StringBase._interpolate                   102 sites   correct output
  RangeError.checkValidRange                  79 sites   correct output
  StringBuffer.write                          73 sites   correct output
  1043 functions, collision-free            1622 sites   correct output

The last line is the one that matters: 1622 calls dispatched through
Thread::indirect_static_call_table_ and every one arrived. A mapping over all
1106 functions instead overflows the stack, and that is the fixture, not the
mechanism -- 63 kernel offsets are shared by more than one function, so
several land on one slot and calls to one arrive at another.

81 of 81 shorebird tests, verify_reproduction.sh clean at 24 artifacts and 14
stubs, dd_pass_check.sh clean.

Co-Authored-By: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>

